WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.209 --> 00:00:07.712
Human language, mathematics, logic.

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:10.423
These are all ways to formalize the world.

00:00:10.465 --> 00:00:12.175
And in our century,

00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:15.554
there's a new and yet
more powerful one: computation.

00:00:16.263 --> 00:00:17.430
For nearly 50 years,

00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:19.140
I've had the great privilege

00:00:19.182 --> 00:00:22.727
of building up an ever-taller tower
of science and technology

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:24.938
that's based on that idea of computation.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.401
And so today, I want to tell you
a little bit about what that's led to.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:32.529
There's a lot to talk about,
so I'm going to go quickly.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:34.948
And sometimes I'm going
to summarize in a sentence

00:00:34.948 --> 00:00:36.741
what I've written a whole book about.

00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:38.868
But you know,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.705
I last gave a TED talk 13 years ago,

00:00:41.705 --> 00:00:43.206
in February 2010,

00:00:43.248 --> 00:00:45.333
soon after WolframAlpha launched,

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:47.836
and I ended that talk with a question.

00:00:47.877 --> 00:00:49.045
Question was,

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:52.215
is computation ultimately
what's underneath everything

00:00:52.215 --> 00:00:53.800
in our universe?

00:00:53.842 --> 00:00:56.428
I gave myself a decade to find out.

00:00:56.428 --> 00:00:58.930
And actually, it could
have needed a century.

00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:02.726
But in April 2020,
just after the decade mark,

00:01:02.726 --> 00:01:04.603
we were thrilled to be able to announce

00:01:04.644 --> 00:01:08.440
what seems to be the ultimate
machine code of the universe.

00:01:08.481 --> 00:01:10.859
And yes, it's computational.

00:01:11.276 --> 00:01:14.529
So computation isn't just
a possible formalization,

00:01:14.529 --> 00:01:17.240
it's the ultimate one for our universe.

00:01:18.074 --> 00:01:24.080
It all starts from the idea that space,
like matter, is made of discrete elements,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.793
and from that structure of space
and everything in it,

00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:31.921
it's defined just
by a network of relations

00:01:31.963 --> 00:01:35.300
between these elements
that we might call atoms of space.

00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:39.012
So it's all very elegant,
but deeply abstract.

00:01:39.721 --> 00:01:42.349
But here's kind of
a humanized representation,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.934
a version of the very beginning
of the universe.

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:47.687
And what we're seeing here
is the emergence of space

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.022
and everything in it

00:01:49.022 --> 00:01:52.108
by the successive application
of very simple computational rules.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:56.154
And remember, these dots
are not atoms in any existing space.

00:01:56.196 --> 00:02:00.700
They're atoms of space
that get put together to make space.

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:03.328
And yes, if we kept going long enough,

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.622
we could build
our whole universe this way.

00:02:06.122 --> 00:02:08.333
So eons later,

00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:11.753
here's a chunk of space
with two little black holes

00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:16.716
that, if we wait a little while,
will eventually merge,

00:02:16.758 --> 00:02:19.844
generating little ripples
of gravitational radiation.

00:02:20.512 --> 00:02:24.099
And remember, all of this is built
from pure computation.

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:27.602
But like fluid mechanics
emerging from molecules,

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:32.107
what emerges here is space-time
and Einstein's equations for gravity,

00:02:32.148 --> 00:02:35.485
though there are deviations
that we just might be able to detect,

00:02:35.527 --> 00:02:39.781
like that the dimensionality of space
won't always be precisely three.

00:02:40.573 --> 00:02:42.033
And there's something else.

00:02:42.075 --> 00:02:46.663
Our computational rules can inevitably
be applied in many ways,

00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:49.207
each defining a different
kind of thread of time,

00:02:49.207 --> 00:02:52.752
a different path of history
that can branch and merge.

00:02:53.128 --> 00:02:56.005
But as observers
embedded in this universe,

00:02:56.005 --> 00:02:57.924
we're branching and merging, too.

00:02:57.966 --> 00:03:01.469
And it turns out that quantum mechanics
emerges as the story

00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:05.348
of how branching minds
perceive a branching universe.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.934
So the little pink lines
you might be able to see here

00:03:07.976 --> 00:03:10.562
show the structure
of what we call branchial space,

00:03:10.562 --> 00:03:12.522
the space of quantum branches.

00:03:12.564 --> 00:03:14.649
And one of the stunningly
beautiful things,

00:03:14.691 --> 00:03:16.359
at least for physicists like me,

00:03:16.359 --> 00:03:20.989
is that the same phenomenon
that in physical space gives us gravity,

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:23.658
in branchial space gives us
quantum mechanics.

00:03:24.701 --> 00:03:26.953
So in the history of science so far,

00:03:26.953 --> 00:03:30.582
I think we can identify
sort of four broad paradigms

00:03:30.582 --> 00:03:33.209
for making models of the world
that can be distinguished

00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:35.587
kind of by how they deal with time.

00:03:35.587 --> 00:03:39.466
So in antiquity and in plenty
of areas of science, even today,

00:03:39.507 --> 00:03:41.926
it's all about kind of,
what are things made of.

00:03:41.926 --> 00:03:43.553
And time doesn't really enter.

00:03:43.887 --> 00:03:47.015
But in the 1600s came the idea
of modeling things

00:03:47.015 --> 00:03:50.185
with mathematical formulas
in which time enters,

00:03:50.226 --> 00:03:52.479
but basically just as a coordinate value.

00:03:53.062 --> 00:03:57.066
Then in the 1980s, and this is something
in which I was deeply involved,

00:03:57.108 --> 00:03:59.360
came the idea of making models

00:03:59.402 --> 00:04:01.863
by starting with simple
computational rules

00:04:01.863 --> 00:04:03.698
and just letting them run.

00:04:03.698 --> 00:04:06.117
So can one predict what will happen?

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:07.869
No.

00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:10.205
There's what I call
computational irreducibility,

00:04:10.246 --> 00:04:15.126
in which, in effect, the passage of time
corresponds to an irreducible computation

00:04:15.126 --> 00:04:18.588
that we have to run in order to work out
how it will turn out.

00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:20.423
But now there's kind of something,

00:04:20.423 --> 00:04:23.593
something even more --
in our physics project,

00:04:23.593 --> 00:04:26.054
there’s things that have become
multi-computational,

00:04:26.054 --> 00:04:27.764
with many threads of time

00:04:27.764 --> 00:04:31.267
that can only be knitted
together by an observer.

00:04:31.309 --> 00:04:34.854
So it's kind of a new paradigm
that actually seems to unlock things

00:04:34.854 --> 00:04:36.689
not only in fundamental physics,

00:04:36.731 --> 00:04:39.901
but also in the foundations of mathematics
and computer science,

00:04:39.901 --> 00:04:43.196
and possibly in areas like biology
and economics as well.

00:04:44.364 --> 00:04:46.533
So I talked about building up the universe

00:04:46.574 --> 00:04:49.160
by repeatedly applying
a computational rule.

00:04:49.202 --> 00:04:51.454
But how is that rule picked?

00:04:51.496 --> 00:04:53.289
Well, actually it isn't,

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:55.750
because all possible rules are used,

00:04:55.792 --> 00:04:58.002
and we're building up
what I call the ruliad,

00:04:58.002 --> 00:05:00.922
the kind of deeply abstract
but unique object

00:05:00.964 --> 00:05:05.009
that is the entangled limit
of all possible computational processes.

00:05:05.635 --> 00:05:09.722
Here's a tiny fragment of it
shown in terms of Turing machines.

00:05:09.764 --> 00:05:13.309
So this ruliad is everything.

00:05:13.309 --> 00:05:16.729
And we as observers
are necessarily part of it.

00:05:17.063 --> 00:05:18.648
In the ruliad as a whole,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:22.193
in a sense, everything
computationally possible can happen.

00:05:22.235 --> 00:05:26.906
But observers like us just sample
specific slices of the ruliad.

00:05:26.906 --> 00:05:29.742
And there are two crucial facts about us.

00:05:29.784 --> 00:05:33.663
First, we're computationally bounded,
our minds are limited,

00:05:33.663 --> 00:05:36.416
and second, we believe
we're persistent in time,

00:05:36.457 --> 00:05:39.794
even though we're made of different
atoms of space at every moment.

00:05:39.836 --> 00:05:41.880
So then, here's the big result.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.633
What observers with those characteristics
perceive in the ruliad

00:05:45.675 --> 00:05:48.052
necessarily follows certain laws.

00:05:48.052 --> 00:05:50.471
And those laws turn out to be precisely

00:05:50.513 --> 00:05:53.308
the three key theories
of 20th century physics:

00:05:53.308 --> 00:05:55.643
general relativity, quantum mechanics,

00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:58.271
and statistical mechanics
in the second law.

00:05:58.313 --> 00:06:01.482
So it's because we're observers like us

00:06:01.524 --> 00:06:04.360
that we perceive
the laws of physics we do.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.696
We can think of sort of different minds

00:06:06.738 --> 00:06:09.532
as being at different places
in rulial space.

00:06:09.574 --> 00:06:11.826
Human minds who think alike are nearby,

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:13.328
animals further away,

00:06:13.369 --> 00:06:15.622
and further out, we get
to kind of alien minds

00:06:15.663 --> 00:06:17.624
where it's hard to make a translation.

00:06:18.041 --> 00:06:20.835
So how can we get intuition
for all of this?

00:06:20.835 --> 00:06:23.379
Well, one thing we can do
is use generative AI

00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:26.633
to take what amounts to an incredibly
tiny slice of the ruliad

00:06:26.674 --> 00:06:30.011
aligned with images
we humans have produced.

00:06:30.011 --> 00:06:32.805
We can think of this
as sort of a place in the ruliad

00:06:32.847 --> 00:06:36.851
described by using the concept
of a cat in a party hat.

00:06:37.435 --> 00:06:40.521
So zooming out,
we saw there

00:06:40.521 --> 00:06:42.523
what we might call Cat Island.

00:06:42.523 --> 00:06:45.568
Pretty soon we’re in a kind of
an inter-concept space.

00:06:45.568 --> 00:06:47.445
Occasionally things will look familiar,

00:06:47.487 --> 00:06:51.908
but mostly, what we'll see is things
we humans don't have words for.

00:06:52.283 --> 00:06:54.744
In physical space, we explore the universe

00:06:54.744 --> 00:06:56.704
by sending out spacecraft.

00:06:56.704 --> 00:06:59.040
In rulial space, we explore more

00:06:59.082 --> 00:07:01.918
by expanding our concepts
and our paradigms.

00:07:02.335 --> 00:07:04.504
We can kind of get a sense
of what's out there

00:07:04.504 --> 00:07:06.255
by sampling possible rules,

00:07:06.255 --> 00:07:08.383
doing what I call ruliology.

00:07:08.424 --> 00:07:10.635
So even with incredibly simple rules,

00:07:10.677 --> 00:07:12.470
there's incredible richness.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:15.264
But the issue is that most of it
doesn't yet connect

00:07:15.306 --> 00:07:18.476
with things we humans
understand or care about.

00:07:18.476 --> 00:07:20.561
It's like when we look
at the natural world

00:07:20.561 --> 00:07:24.273
and only gradually realize that we can
use features of it for technology.

00:07:24.649 --> 00:07:27.652
So even after everything
our civilization has achieved,

00:07:27.694 --> 00:07:31.572
we're just at the very, very beginning
of exploring rulial space.

00:07:31.614 --> 00:07:33.116
What about AIs?

00:07:33.157 --> 00:07:35.368
Well, just like we can do ruliology,

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.830
AIs can in principle go out
and explore rulial space.

00:07:38.871 --> 00:07:40.707
Left to their own devices, though,

00:07:40.707 --> 00:07:42.417
they'll mostly just be doing things

00:07:42.417 --> 00:07:45.211
we humans don't connect with
or care about.

00:07:45.545 --> 00:07:47.922
So the big achievements
of AI in recent times

00:07:47.964 --> 00:07:51.384
have been about making systems
that are closely aligned with us humans.

00:07:51.426 --> 00:07:54.846
We train LLMs on billions of web pages
so they can produce texts

00:07:54.887 --> 00:07:57.098
that's typical of what we humans write.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.975
And yes, the fact that this works

00:07:59.017 --> 00:08:01.936
is undoubtedly telling us
some deep scientific things

00:08:01.978 --> 00:08:04.230
about the semantic grammar of language

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.357
and generalizations of things like logic

00:08:06.357 --> 00:08:08.651
that perhaps we should
have known centuries ago.

00:08:08.693 --> 00:08:10.570
You know, for much of human history,

00:08:10.611 --> 00:08:12.822
we were kind of like the LLMs,

00:08:12.822 --> 00:08:16.409
figuring things out by kind of matching
patterns in our minds.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:20.538
But then came more systematic
formalization and eventually computation.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:24.876
And with that, we got a whole other level
of power to truly create new things

00:08:24.917 --> 00:08:28.296
and to, in effect, go wherever
we want in the ruliad.

00:08:28.337 --> 00:08:32.175
But the challenge is to do that in a way
that connects with what we humans,

00:08:32.216 --> 00:08:34.927
and our AIs, understand.

00:08:34.969 --> 00:08:37.180
In fact, I've devoted
a large part of my life

00:08:37.221 --> 00:08:39.515
to kind of trying to build that bridge.

00:08:39.515 --> 00:08:43.728
It's all been about creating a language
for expressing ourselves computationally,

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.772
a language for computational thinking.

00:08:46.355 --> 00:08:51.110
The goal is to formalize what we know
about the world in computational terms,

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:54.739
to have computational ways to represent
cities and chemicals and movies

00:08:54.739 --> 00:08:57.992
and humor and formulas
and our knowledge about them.

00:08:58.951 --> 00:09:03.206
It’s been a vast undertaking that spanned
more than four decades of my life,

00:09:03.206 --> 00:09:05.708
but it's something
very unique and different.

00:09:05.708 --> 00:09:08.878
But I'm happy to report
that in what has been Mathematica

00:09:08.878 --> 00:09:10.463
and is now the Wolfram Language,

00:09:10.463 --> 00:09:13.466
I think we firmly succeeded in creating

00:09:13.466 --> 00:09:16.219
a truly full-scale computational language.

00:09:16.677 --> 00:09:17.845
In effect,

00:09:17.845 --> 00:09:21.140
every one of these functions here
can be thought of as formalizing

00:09:21.182 --> 00:09:23.768
and encapsulating, in computational terms,

00:09:23.768 --> 00:09:27.146
some facet of the intellectual
achievements of our civilization.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.234
It's sort of the most concentrated form
of intellectual expression that I know,

00:09:31.275 --> 00:09:34.862
sort of finding the essence of everything
and coherently expressing it

00:09:34.904 --> 00:09:37.031
in the design of our
computational language.

00:09:37.365 --> 00:09:38.533
For me personally,

00:09:38.533 --> 00:09:41.119
it's been an amazing journey,
kind of, year after year,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.247
building the sort of tower of ideas
and technology that's needed.

00:09:44.247 --> 00:09:46.666
And nowadays sharing
that process with the world

00:09:46.707 --> 00:09:48.918
in things like open
live streams and so on.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:50.920
A few centuries ago,

00:09:50.962 --> 00:09:53.172
the development of mathematical notation,

00:09:53.214 --> 00:09:55.883
and what amounts
to the language of mathematics,

00:09:55.925 --> 00:10:01.139
gave a systematic way to express math
and made possible algebra and calculus,

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:04.684
and eventually all of modern
mathematical science.

00:10:04.684 --> 00:10:07.937
And computational language now
provides a similar path,

00:10:07.979 --> 00:10:11.149
letting us ultimately create
a computational X

00:10:11.149 --> 00:10:13.651
for all imaginable fields X.

00:10:14.026 --> 00:10:17.363
I mean, we've seen the growth
of computer science, CS,

00:10:17.405 --> 00:10:20.783
but computational language opens up
something ultimately much bigger

00:10:20.825 --> 00:10:23.244
and broader, CX.

00:10:23.244 --> 00:10:25.705
I mean, for 70 years
we've had programming languages

00:10:25.746 --> 00:10:29.542
which are about telling computers
in their terms what to do.

00:10:29.584 --> 00:10:30.835
But computational language

00:10:30.877 --> 00:10:33.045
is about something
intellectually much bigger.

00:10:33.045 --> 00:10:35.464
It's about taking everything
we can think about

00:10:35.506 --> 00:10:38.593
and operationalizing it
in computational terms.

00:10:38.968 --> 00:10:40.845
You know, I built the Wolfram Language

00:10:40.887 --> 00:10:43.389
first and foremost
because I wanted to use it myself.

00:10:43.431 --> 00:10:44.765
And now when I use it,

00:10:44.807 --> 00:10:47.602
I feel like it's kind of giving me
some kind of superpower.

00:10:47.602 --> 00:10:51.189
I just have to imagine something
in computational terms.

00:10:51.189 --> 00:10:54.984
And then the language sort of almost
magically lets me bring it into reality,

00:10:55.026 --> 00:10:57.320
see its consequences, and build on them.

00:10:57.361 --> 00:10:59.197
And yes, that's the sort of superpower

00:10:59.197 --> 00:11:01.532
that's let me do things
like our physics project.

00:11:01.532 --> 00:11:03.201
And over the past 35 years,

00:11:03.242 --> 00:11:07.622
it's been my great privilege to share
this superpower with many other people,

00:11:07.622 --> 00:11:08.831
and by doing so,

00:11:08.831 --> 00:11:12.835
to have enabled an incredible number
of advances across many fields.

00:11:13.252 --> 00:11:17.465
It's sort of a wonderful thing to see
people, researchers, CEOs, kids,

00:11:17.465 --> 00:11:20.927
using our language to fluently
think in computational terms,

00:11:20.968 --> 00:11:23.387
kind of crispening up their own thinking,

00:11:23.387 --> 00:11:27.099
and then in effect, automatically
calling in computational superpowers.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.894
And now it's not just people
who can do that.

00:11:29.936 --> 00:11:33.272
AIs can use our computational
language as a tool, too.

00:11:33.272 --> 00:11:35.524
Yes, to get their facts straight,

00:11:35.566 --> 00:11:38.569
but even more importantly,
to compute new facts.

00:11:38.861 --> 00:11:42.031
There are already some integrations
of our technology into LLMs.

00:11:42.073 --> 00:11:44.116
There's a lot more you'll be seeing soon.

00:11:44.116 --> 00:11:46.535
And, you know, when it comes
to building new things

00:11:46.577 --> 00:11:48.704
in a very powerful emerging workflow,

00:11:48.704 --> 00:11:53.084
it's basically to start by telling
the LLM roughly what you want,

00:11:53.084 --> 00:11:56.420
then to have it try to express that
in precise Wolfram Language,

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:59.590
then, and this is a critical feature
of our computational language,

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:01.801
compared to, for example,
programming language,

00:12:01.842 --> 00:12:04.011
you as a human can read the code,

00:12:04.011 --> 00:12:05.429
and if it does what you want,

00:12:05.471 --> 00:12:08.516
you can use it as kind of
a dependable component to build on.

00:12:08.891 --> 00:12:11.310
OK, but let's say we use more and more AI,

00:12:11.352 --> 00:12:13.020
more and more computation.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:14.814
What's the world going to be like?

00:12:14.814 --> 00:12:16.899
From the industrial revolution on,

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:19.777
we’ve been used to doing engineering
where we can in effect,

00:12:19.777 --> 00:12:23.531
see how the gears mesh
to understand how things work.

00:12:23.572 --> 00:12:25.408
But computational irreducibility

00:12:25.449 --> 00:12:28.077
now shows us that that won't
always be possible.

00:12:28.119 --> 00:12:31.330
We won't always be able to make
a kind of simple human or, say,

00:12:31.372 --> 00:12:33.040
mathematical narrative

00:12:33.082 --> 00:12:35.584
to explain or predict
what a system will do.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:40.089
And yes, this is science, in effect,
eating itself from the inside.

00:12:40.548 --> 00:12:42.842
From all the successes
of mathematical science,

00:12:42.883 --> 00:12:46.554
we've come to believe that somehow,
if we only could find them,

00:12:46.595 --> 00:12:49.765
there'd be formulas
to kind of predict everything.

00:12:50.141 --> 00:12:53.936
But now computational irreducibility
shows us that that isn't true.

00:12:54.312 --> 00:12:56.981
And that in effect, to find out
what a system will do,

00:12:56.981 --> 00:13:00.609
we have to go through the same
irreducible computational steps

00:13:00.651 --> 00:13:02.236
as the system itself.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.447
Yes, it's a weakness of science,

00:13:04.447 --> 00:13:08.451
but it's also why the passage of time
is significant and meaningful

00:13:08.492 --> 00:13:12.330
and why we can't just sort of
jump ahead to get the answer.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.206
We have to live the steps.

00:13:14.749 --> 00:13:18.627
It's actually going to be, I think,
a great societal dilemma of the future.

00:13:18.627 --> 00:13:22.465
If we let our AIs achieve their
kind of full computational potential,

00:13:22.465 --> 00:13:24.800
they'll have lots
of computational irreducibility

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.094
and we won't be able
to predict what they'll do.

00:13:27.136 --> 00:13:30.431
But if we put constraints on them
to make them more predictable,

00:13:30.473 --> 00:13:32.475
we'll limit what they can do for us.

00:13:32.808 --> 00:13:37.813
So what will it feel like if our world
is full of computational irreducibility?

00:13:38.189 --> 00:13:40.358
Well, it's really nothing new

00:13:40.399 --> 00:13:42.818
because that's the story
with much of nature.

00:13:42.818 --> 00:13:44.028
And what's happened there

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.655
is that we've found ways
to operate within nature,

00:13:46.697 --> 00:13:49.450
even though nature
can sometimes still surprise us.

00:13:49.784 --> 00:13:51.786
And so it will be with the AIs.

00:13:51.827 --> 00:13:55.289
We might give them a constitution,
but there will always be consequences

00:13:55.331 --> 00:13:56.540
we can't predict.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:01.379
Of course, even figuring out societally
what we want from the AIs is hard.

00:14:01.379 --> 00:14:03.422
Maybe we need you know, a promptocracy

00:14:03.422 --> 00:14:06.217
where people write prompts
instead of just voting.

00:14:06.801 --> 00:14:10.137
But basically, every control
the outcome scheme

00:14:10.179 --> 00:14:12.306
seems full of both political philosophy

00:14:12.348 --> 00:14:15.017
and computational irreducibility gotchas.

00:14:15.559 --> 00:14:18.479
You know, if we look
at the whole arc of human history,

00:14:18.479 --> 00:14:20.689
the one thing that's
systematically changed

00:14:20.689 --> 00:14:22.775
is that more and more gets automated.

00:14:22.775 --> 00:14:26.278
And LLMs just gave us a dramatic
and unexpected example of that.

00:14:26.821 --> 00:14:27.988
So what does that mean?

00:14:27.988 --> 00:14:31.158
Does that mean that in the end,
us humans will have nothing to do?

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:33.452
Well, if we look at history,

00:14:33.494 --> 00:14:36.831
what seems to happen is that when
one thing gets automated away,

00:14:36.872 --> 00:14:39.375
it opens up lots of new things to do.

00:14:39.417 --> 00:14:41.043
And as economies develop,

00:14:41.085 --> 00:14:45.464
the pie chart of occupations
seems to get more and more fragmented.

00:14:45.881 --> 00:14:47.591
And now we're back to the ruliad.

00:14:47.591 --> 00:14:49.135
Because at a foundational level,

00:14:49.176 --> 00:14:52.346
what's happening is that automation
is opening up more directions

00:14:52.388 --> 00:14:53.848
to go in the ruliad.

00:14:53.848 --> 00:14:56.851
But there's no abstract way
to choose between these.

00:14:56.892 --> 00:14:59.019
It's a question of what we humans want,

00:14:59.061 --> 00:15:02.815
and it requires kind of humans
doing work to define that.

00:15:02.815 --> 00:15:07.611
So a society of AI as sort of
untethered by human input,

00:15:07.653 --> 00:15:10.406
would effectively go off
and explore the whole ruliad.

00:15:10.406 --> 00:15:14.535
But most of what they do would seem
to us random and pointless,

00:15:14.577 --> 00:15:18.289
much like most of nature
doesn't seem to us right now,

00:15:18.289 --> 00:15:20.040
like it's achieving a purpose.

00:15:20.541 --> 00:15:24.962
I mean, one used to imagine that to build
things that are useful to us,

00:15:25.004 --> 00:15:27.298
we'd have to do it kind of step by step.

00:15:27.673 --> 00:15:30.384
But AI and the whole
phenomenon of computation

00:15:30.426 --> 00:15:32.386
tell us that really what we need

00:15:32.428 --> 00:15:34.805
is more just to define what we want.

00:15:35.306 --> 00:15:39.727
Then computation, AI,
automation can make it happen.

00:15:39.768 --> 00:15:43.522
And yes, I think the key to defining
in a clear way what we want

00:15:43.564 --> 00:15:45.357
is computational language.

00:15:45.357 --> 00:15:47.526
And, you know, even after 35 years,

00:15:47.526 --> 00:15:48.736
for many people,

00:15:48.736 --> 00:15:51.697
Wolfram Language is still sort of
an artifact from the future.

00:15:51.697 --> 00:15:54.533
If your job is to program,
it seems like a cheat.

00:15:54.575 --> 00:15:58.120
How come you can do in an hour
what would usually take you a week?

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.249
But it can also be kind of daunting
because having dashed off that one thing,

00:16:02.249 --> 00:16:04.793
you now have to conceptualize
the next thing.

00:16:04.835 --> 00:16:07.296
Of course, it's great for CEOs and CTOs

00:16:07.338 --> 00:16:11.133
and intellectual leaders who are ready
to race on to the next thing.

00:16:11.175 --> 00:16:15.804
And indeed, it's an impressively
popular thing in that set.

00:16:16.096 --> 00:16:18.933
In a sense, what's happening
is that Wolfram Language shifts

00:16:18.974 --> 00:16:22.770
from concentrating on mechanics
to concentrating on conceptualization,

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:27.233
and the key to that conceptualization
is broad computational thinking.

00:16:27.233 --> 00:16:29.151
So how can one learn to do that?

00:16:29.193 --> 00:16:31.153
It's not really a story of CS,

00:16:31.195 --> 00:16:33.405
it's really a story of CX.

00:16:33.405 --> 00:16:35.157
And as a kind of education,

00:16:35.199 --> 00:16:37.785
it's more like liberal arts than STEM.

00:16:37.826 --> 00:16:41.413
It's part of a trend that when
you automate technical execution,

00:16:41.413 --> 00:16:45.668
what becomes important
is not figuring out how to do things,

00:16:45.668 --> 00:16:47.086
but what to do.

00:16:47.127 --> 00:16:50.172
And that's more a story
of broad knowledge and general thinking

00:16:50.172 --> 00:16:52.675
than any kind of narrow specialization.

00:16:53.509 --> 00:16:57.012
You know, there's sort of an unexpected
human centeredness to all of this.

00:16:57.054 --> 00:17:00.432
We might have thought that with
the advance of science and technology,

00:17:00.432 --> 00:17:04.144
the particulars of us humans
would become ever less relevant.

00:17:04.144 --> 00:17:07.606
But we've discovered that that's not true,
and that, in fact, everything,

00:17:07.648 --> 00:17:08.857
even our physics,

00:17:08.899 --> 00:17:12.736
depends on how we humans
happen to have sampled the ruliad.

00:17:13.696 --> 00:17:15.739
Before our physics project,

00:17:15.781 --> 00:17:19.159
we didn't know if our universe
really was computational,

00:17:19.201 --> 00:17:21.203
but now it's pretty clear that it is.

00:17:21.245 --> 00:17:23.998
And from that, we're sort of
inexorably led to the ruliad,

00:17:23.998 --> 00:17:25.958
with all its kind of vastness

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:29.378
so hugely greater than the physical
space in our universe.

00:17:29.962 --> 00:17:32.298
So where will we go in the ruliad?

00:17:32.339 --> 00:17:35.551
Computational language
is what lets us chart our path.

00:17:35.551 --> 00:17:38.887
It lets us humans define
our goals and our journeys.

00:17:38.887 --> 00:17:41.515
And what's amazing
is that all the power and depth

00:17:41.557 --> 00:17:44.643
of what's out there in the ruliad
is accessible to everyone.

00:17:45.019 --> 00:17:49.148
One just has to learn to harness
those computational superpowers,

00:17:49.189 --> 00:17:50.733
which kind of starts here,

00:17:50.774 --> 00:17:54.028
you know, our portal to the ruliad.

00:17:54.528 --> 00:17:55.863
Thank you.

00:17:55.904 --> 00:17:59.491
(Applause)

